WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.700
SPEAKER: Hi, I'd like to
show you a cool new way

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.090
to create interactive tutorials
that you can run right

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.532
inside the Cloud
Shell environment.

00:00:08.532 --> 00:00:09.990
So to start with,
let's take a look

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.900
at this GitHub repo under Google
Cloud Platform slash Cloud dash

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.920
Shell dash tutorials.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.660
Now this repo contains a file
called tutorial dot md, which

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.870
is a markdown specification
of the desired structure

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.330
and flow for my tutorial.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.020
Let's look at the underlying
markdown syntax there,

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.140
and we see a few things.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.710
There's one top level
header, and this defines

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.140
the title of my tutorial.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.960
And then there's a sequence
of second level headers

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.430
where each second level header
corresponds to a discrete step

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.040
in my tutorial.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.800
The rest of the file contains
the usual markdown syntax

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.940
formatting, like highlighting
code snippets and command

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.170
lines, and things like that.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.980
There are also a few
special commands,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.860
like this walkthrough
Editor open file.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.110
What this does is it
generates a hyperlink

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.450
in the tutorial,
which, when clicked,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.910
will automatically
open that specified

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:13.500
file from the current repo
in the Cloud Shell Editor.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.009
So that's kind of
roughly the syntax of one

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:16.800
of these tutorials.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.695
Let's see it actually
running live.

00:01:18.695 --> 00:01:21.020
So we'll go back up
to the top level.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.170
And in the Read
Me for this repo,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:29.160
I have a hyperlink coded,
which runs that exact file.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.940
So what this does is it
specifies the Cloud Shell URL.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.100
It tells the system
which repo I would

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.440
like automatically cloned.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.150
It says I'd like the Cloud
Shell Editor enabled by default.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.330
And the new piece here is this
tutorial equals parameter,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.020
which defines a file in
this repo to be interpreted

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.910
as a tutorial in markdown.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.730
So we're directing it
at the tutorial dot md

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.710
file we just looked at.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.890
Let's click on that link
and see what happens.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.340
So it's warning me it's
about to auto-clone

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.560
a repo into my environment.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.370
It instantiates the
Shell, clones that repo,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.570
starts up the Cloud Shell Editor
with all the files in that repo

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.270
as a project, and
it automatically

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.210
started my tutorial on the
right-hand side of the screen.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.660
As you can see, I have
my title and my steps

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.460
that I can go forward
and backward through.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.650
It highlights some of the
syntax, like this Echo command.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.470
You get an automatic
button to copy that

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:33.180
to the clipboard, which I can
then paste into a Cloud Shell.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.920
So a lot of nice authoring
capabilities there.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.562
And I also have those
special integrated directives

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:39.270
I told you about.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.450
For example, if you
wanted to direct somebody

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.300
to click on this
Web Preview button,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.940
rather than kind of direct
them there through imagery

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.840
or instructions, you can
generate one of these URL--

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.600
or one of these hyperlinks,
which, when clicked,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.112
will automatically spotlight
that portion of the screen.

00:02:58.112 --> 00:02:59.820
As you go through to
the end you can even

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.430
generate a little widget
that gets feedback

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.770
from the user as to what
their experience was

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.200
like with the tutorial.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.760
One last thing I
want to show you

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.550
is that you can actually
run these tutorials right

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.970
from the Shell.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.070
And what I'm going to do
is finish the tutorial,

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:22.030
close it, and then invoke
it again from over here.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.640
All I had to do was run
Cloud Shell, launch tutorial,

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.390
minus D for developer
mode, and then

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.940
the file in this
current environment

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.230
that I want to be the
specification of the tutorial.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.240
And that starts it
just like the hyperlink

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.440
did in my previous example.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.650
So this is a nice way
if you're an author

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.430
and you want to test and
iterate on a tutorial,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.960
this gives you the ability to
do that right from the console.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.590
That's pretty much it.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.100
I also wanted to point you
to the documentation, which

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.398
you can find under
Cloud dot Google dot com

00:03:55.398 --> 00:03:59.730
slash Shell slash
docs slash tutorials.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.470
And there you can
find more details

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.680
about formatting
your markdown and all

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.750
of the different capabilities
that are available,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.250
those special
features I mentioned

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.270
for integrating your tutorial
with the console or the Cloud

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.140
Shell environment.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:14.690
So that's it.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:15.690
I hope you enjoyed that.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.356
I hope this gave you a sense for
some of the powerful things you

00:04:18.356 --> 00:04:21.149
can do in authoring these
tutorials in the Google Cloud

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:21.899
environment.

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:23.449
Thanks.

